{:edb/db-before-t 1180 :edb/db-after-t 1181 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534493 50 #inst "2026-10-07T08:48:38.339Z" 13194139534493 true] [17592186115291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291785 13194139534493 true] [17592186115291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291789 13194139534493 true] [17592186115291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291799 13194139534493 true] [17592186115292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291792 13194139534493 true] [17592186115292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186291796 13194139534493 true] [17592186291785 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291786 13194139534493 true] [17592186291785 1089 "q-339080" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291785 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534493 true] [17592186291785 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {7}^{-x})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291785 1113 #uuid "0ed23e01-3b8b-53d3-bf5f-2d1f34d8be1d" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291785 1145 "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {7}^{-x}) & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{({7}^{-1})}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/assets/f2858b7d6adc07c0f2196bbe8170f17fa02aff19d494726c08f7c03a419afd53.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function approaches zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}=\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 0=0.\n$$" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291786 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291787 13194139534493 true] [17592186291786 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291788 13194139534493 true] [17592186291786 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534493 true] [17592186291786 1041 "field-1" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291786 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291788 13194139534493 true] [17592186291786 1110 #uuid "78087df0-84a9-5dca-8f45-8065bf21cea3" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291787 1034 "1" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291787 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534493 true] [17592186291787 1111 #uuid "8186550d-859c-5feb-b891-cd2519c7cb4f" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291788 1034 "0" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291788 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534493 true] [17592186291788 1111 #uuid "2dc46f4c-7311-51be-a7ec-48d06c7c434b" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291789 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291790 13194139534493 true] [17592186291789 1089 "q-339407" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291789 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534493 true] [17592186291789 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291789 1113 #uuid "685bf91b-cfa0-5d3f-9ff7-84b24dff711f" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291789 1145 "We recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{8})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/assets/99c2329c99005af33f4b62d61547e18dcaa7f04afc931df57439bd1ca08ff35c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291790 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291791 13194139534493 true] [17592186291790 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534493 true] [17592186291790 1041 "field-1" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291790 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291791 13194139534493 true] [17592186291790 1110 #uuid "9c467857-1a0b-5c78-84d7-b6e7d4e72f00" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291791 1034 "\\infty" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291791 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534493 true] [17592186291791 1111 #uuid "dc93ab5a-2fc6-5638-8e00-85569d3ed330" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291792 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291793 13194139534493 true] [17592186291792 1089 "q-339411" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291792 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534493 true] [17592186291792 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2\\cdot {e}^{4x-3}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291792 1113 #uuid "0009578d-6d2c-52a3-8af9-429c07a34403" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291792 1145 "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2\\cdot {e}^{4x-3} & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{4x-3} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{-3}\\cdot {e}^{4x}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {e}^{-3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{4x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{4})^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={({e}^{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/assets/6590b80777c49b3d018fa38ce79c7fb14103365f783dac18135b8d3637ee724d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{4})^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe negative factor $-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{4})^{x}=-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}\\cdot \\infty =-\\infty .\n$$" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291793 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291794 13194139534493 true] [17592186291793 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291795 13194139534493 true] [17592186291793 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534493 true] [17592186291793 1041 "field-1" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291793 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291795 13194139534493 true] [17592186291793 1110 #uuid "132f82cc-a838-5394-a0bf-b4439a4a55cf" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291794 1034 "0" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291794 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534493 true] [17592186291794 1111 #uuid "3eecbe5d-5169-5ecb-8d66-cf684eafd2b6" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291795 1034 "-\\infty" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291795 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534493 true] [17592186291795 1111 #uuid "8d57480f-e01f-5395-8319-9798c6e73a71" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291796 1017 #edb/ref 17592186291797 13194139534493 true] [17592186291796 1089 "q-339413" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291796 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534493 true] [17592186291796 1100 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{3-2x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291796 1113 #uuid "6fa418ff-7c76-5ae9-aad5-d6dfb417acfd" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291796 1145 "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{3-2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({5}^{3}\\cdot {5}^{-2x}) \\\\  & ={5}^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{-2x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{({5}^{-2})}^{x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{{5}^{2}})}^{x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{25})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{25})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013814/assets/3795890dac6f542c8aa75d61deb8a03192a41532270b74eafcd972e82ac6721e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{25})}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{3-2x}=125\\cdot 0=0.\n$$" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291797 1000 #edb/ref 17592186291798 13194139534493 true] [17592186291797 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534493 true] [17592186291797 1041 "field-1" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291797 1104 #edb/ref 17592186291798 13194139534493 true] [17592186291797 1110 #uuid "ab1b0323-a9a4-50a1-93aa-709f148ea69d" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291798 1034 "0" 13194139534493 true] [17592186291798 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534493 true] [17592